    {
      "id": "d70514339ef3-q4",
      "number": 4,
      "question": "The equation F= k(q 1 x q 2 /q 1 ) is representation of: (Where F is the attractive or repulsive electric force of two point charges q 1 and q 2 and r is the distance between two charges) &#2360;&#2350;&#2368;&#2325;&#2352;&#2339; F= k(q 1 x q 2 /q 1 ) &#2344;&#2367;&#2350;&#2381;&#2344; &#2325;&#2366; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2340;&#2367;&#2344;&#2367;&#2343;&#2367;&#2340;&#2381;&#2357; &#2325;&#2352;&#2340;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376;: (&#2332;&#2361;&#2366;&#2305; F &#2342;&#2379; &#2348;&#2367;&#2344;&#2381;&#2342;&#2369; &#2310;&#2357;&#2375;&#2358;&#2379;&#2306; q 1 &#2340;&#2341;&#2366; q 2 &#2325;&#2366; &#2310;&#2325;&#2352;&#2381;&#2359;&#2325; &#2351;&#2366; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2340;&#2367;&#2325;&#2352;&#2381;&#2359;&#2368; &#2357;&#2367;&#2342;&#2381;&#2351;&#2369;&#2340; &#2348;&#2354; &#2361;&#2376; &#2340;&#2341;&#2366; r &#2342;&#2379; &#2310;&#2357;&#2375;&#2358;&#2379;&#2306; &#2325;&#2375; &#2348;&#2368;&#2330; &#2325;&#2368; &#2342;&#2370;&#2352;&#2368; &#2361;&#2376;)",
      "questionHtml": "<span class=\"eqt\"><p>The equation F= k(q<sub>1</sub> x q<sub>2</sub>/q<sub>1</sub>) is representation of: </p><p>(Where F is the attractive or repulsive electric force of two point charges q<sub>1</sub> and q<sub>2</sub> and r is the distance between two charges)</p></span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\"><p>&#2360;&#2350;&#2368;&#2325;&#2352;&#2339; F= k(q<sub>1</sub> x q<sub>2</sub>/q<sub>1</sub>) &#2344;&#2367;&#2350;&#2381;&#2344; &#2325;&#2366; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2340;&#2367;&#2344;&#2367;&#2343;&#2367;&#2340;&#2381;&#2357; &#2325;&#2352;&#2340;&#2366; &#2361;&#2376;:</p><p>(&#2332;&#2361;&#2366;&#2305; F &#2342;&#2379; &#2348;&#2367;&#2344;&#2381;&#2342;&#2369; &#2310;&#2357;&#2375;&#2358;&#2379;&#2306; q<sub>1</sub> &#2340;&#2341;&#2366; q<sub>2</sub> &#2325;&#2366; &#2310;&#2325;&#2352;&#2381;&#2359;&#2325; &#2351;&#2366; &#2346;&#2381;&#2352;&#2340;&#2367;&#2325;&#2352;&#2381;&#2359;&#2368; &#2357;&#2367;&#2342;&#2381;&#2351;&#2369;&#2340; &#2348;&#2354; &#2361;&#2376; &#2340;&#2341;&#2366; r &#2342;&#2379; &#2310;&#2357;&#2375;&#2358;&#2379;&#2306; &#2325;&#2375; &#2348;&#2368;&#2330; &#2325;&#2368; &#2342;&#2370;&#2352;&#2368; &#2361;&#2376;)</p></span>",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "A",
          "text": "Coulomb's law &#2325;&#2370;&#2354;&#2377;&#2350; &#2325;&#2366; &#2344;&#2367;&#2351;&#2350;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Coulomb's law</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2325;&#2370;&#2354;&#2377;&#2350; &#2325;&#2366; &#2344;&#2367;&#2351;&#2350;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "B",
          "text": "Biot-Savart law &#2348;&#2366;&#2351;&#2379;&#2335;-&#2360;&#2366;&#2357;&#2352;&#2381;&#2340; &#2344;&#2367;&#2351;&#2350;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Biot-Savart law</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2348;&#2366;&#2351;&#2379;&#2335;-&#2360;&#2366;&#2357;&#2352;&#2381;&#2340; &#2344;&#2367;&#2351;&#2350;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "C",
          "text": "Charle's law &#2330;&#2366;&#2352;&#2381;&#2354;&#2381;&#2360; &#2325;&#2366; &#2344;&#2367;&#2351;&#2350;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Charle's law</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2330;&#2366;&#2352;&#2381;&#2354;&#2381;&#2360; &#2325;&#2366; &#2344;&#2367;&#2351;&#2350;</span>"
        },
        {
          "id": "D",
          "text": "Ohm's law &#2323;&#2350; &#2325;&#2366;&#2344;&#2370;&#2344;",
          "html": "<span class=\"eqt\">Ohm's law</span><span class=\"hqt\" style=\"display:none\">&#2323;&#2350; &#2325;&#2366;&#2344;&#2370;&#2344;</span>"
        }
      ],
      "correctAnswer": "A",
      "solution": "<p>&bull; Coulomb's Law describes the electrostatic force between two stationary, electrically charged particles.</p><p>&bull; Formulated by French physicist Charles-Augustin de Coulomb in 1785, it states that the magnitude of the electrostatic force (F) between two point charges is directly proportional to the product of the magnitudes of the charges and inversely proportional to the square of the distance (r) between them.&#8203;</p>",
